      "question": {
        ":question/id": "f11633ad-c3d3-53f8-93e6-d3a1d7d686df",
        ":question/problem": "$\\int x\\left(- x^{2} + 11\\right)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "In this case, the $x$ outside the parentheses isn't quite the derivative of $(- x^{2} + 11).$ But the derivative is $- 2 x,$ so we're only out by a constant factor.\n\nSo we make the substitution\n\n$$\nu=- x^{2} + 11.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=- 2 x\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad - \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nand we can now write our integral in terms of $u,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int x\\left(- x^{2} + 11\\right)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=- \\frac{1}{2}\\int u^{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve the integral in terms of $u$ and then write the final result in terms of $x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{1}{2}\\int u^{3}\\,\\text{d}u &=- \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{u^{4}}{4}+C\\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{8}u^{4}+C\\\\ &=- \\frac{\\left(- x^{2} + 11\\right)^{4}}{8}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
