      "question": {
        ":question/id": "513c2f70-816a-5ab6-824d-90ff20160823",
        ":question/problem": "Calculate $\\int 2 x^{2}\\sqrt{x^{3} + 13}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nWork on an interval where $x^{3} + 13>0$.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "In this case, the $2 x^{2}$ outside the parentheses isn't quite the derivative of $(x^{3} + 13).$ But the derivative is $3 x^{2},$ so we're only out by a constant factor.\n\nSo we make the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x^{3} + 13.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3 x^{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{d}u=2 x^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nand we can now write our integral in terms of $u,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int 2 x^{2}\\sqrt{x^{3} + 13}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{2}{3}\\int u^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve the integral in terms of $u$ and then write the final result in terms of $x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\int u^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\,\\text{d}u &=\\frac{2}{3}\\frac{u^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{\\frac{3}{2}}+C\\\\ &=\\frac{4}{9}u^{\\frac{3}{2}}+C\\\\ &=\\frac{4 \\left(x^{3} + 13\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{9}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
