        "title": "Integrating an Expression Involving a Power Function Multiplied by a Constant Multiple of Its Derivative"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "4a55f5ea-ce46-5b27-9ca1-d45faffce4c7",
        ":question/problem": "Calculate $\\int x\\sqrt{x^{2} - 7}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nWork on an interval where $x^{2} - 7>0$.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "In this case, the $x$ outside the parentheses isn't quite the derivative of $(x^{2} - 7).$ But the derivative is $2 x,$ so we're only out by a constant factor.\n\nSo we make the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x^{2} - 7.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2 x\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nand we can now write our integral in terms of $u,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int x\\sqrt{x^{2} - 7}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\int u^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve the integral in terms of $u$ and then write the final result in terms of $x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\int u^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\,\\text{d}u &=\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{u^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{\\frac{3}{2}}+C\\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}u^{\\frac{3}{2}}+C\\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(x^{2} - 7\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
